$CATEGORY: LIPPS/Epidemiology/Week 08/Quasi-experimental

::EPI-W8-034::Why is a simple before-after study vulnerable to bias? {
=Other time-related changes can explain the outcome difference#Correct.
~It always randomises exposure#Review intervention-assignment and counterfactual logic.
~It cannot measure outcomes#Review intervention-assignment and counterfactual logic.
~It has too many comparison groups#Review intervention-assignment and counterfactual logic.
}

::EPI-W8-035::Outcome is measured once before and once after intervention with no control group. Which design best fits? {
=Simple before-after#Correct.
~Controlled before-after#Identify whether there is a control group and whether repeated time points are used.
~Interrupted time series#Identify whether there is a control group and whether repeated time points are used.
~Controlled interrupted time series#Identify whether there is a control group and whether repeated time points are used.
}

::EPI-W8-036::Intervention and comparison districts are both measured before and after rollout. Which design best fits? {
=Controlled before-after#Correct.
~Simple before-after#Identify whether there is a control group and whether repeated time points are used.
~Interrupted time series#Identify whether there is a control group and whether repeated time points are used.
~Controlled interrupted time series#Identify whether there is a control group and whether repeated time points are used.
}

::EPI-W8-037::Monthly outcomes are available for 24 months before and 12 months after a policy. Which design best fits? {
=Interrupted time series#Correct.
~Simple before-after#Identify whether there is a control group and whether repeated time points are used.
~Controlled before-after#Identify whether there is a control group and whether repeated time points are used.
~Controlled interrupted time series#Identify whether there is a control group and whether repeated time points are used.
}

